// @check
// @error '__fs_read__' is a compiler intrinsic and cannot be redefined — every call to it lowers to a host import, so this definition would never run
// CONTRACT (must-error): the filesystem floor joins the same reservation the memory tier
// has had since `error-redefine-intrinsic.vl`, and for the same reason — `emitCall`
// matches these names BEFORE it looks up any declaration, so a user function of one of
// them is silently discarded and every call to it becomes a syscall.
//
// The REASON half of the message differs from that fixture's on purpose. The memory
// intrinsics lower to instructions inline; these lower to a `call` of a host import
// declared in the `"imports"` module. Both discard the body, which is what the author
// needs to know, but a message claiming `__fs_read__` "lowers to instructions inline"
// would send the reader looking for an opcode that does not exist.
//
// Reserved, all seven: `__fs_read__`, `__fs_write__`, `__fs_list__`, `__fs_stat__`,
// `__args_count__`, `__args_get__`, `__fs_errno__`. One is exercised here — they share
// a single predicate (`nameIsFsIntrinsic`, a projection of the slot table), so a name in
// the table is reserved by construction rather than by a second list that could drift.
function __fs_read__(path: u8[]): u8[] { return path }
